WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:01.680
Film Courage: Shawn, do you write everyday?

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.650
Shawn Christensen, Co-writer/director THE
VANISHING OF SIDNEY HALL: I do now.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.720
I don’t always.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.059
I’m working on a screenplay that I’m trying
to finalize.

00:00:10.059 --> 00:00:12.990
So yeah, I write everyday.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:26.601
But I’ll definitely go through phases where
I can’t write or I don’t feel like like

00:00:26.601 --> 00:00:28.340
I have the ability to write.

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:33.580
Film Courage: And when you go through that,
do you or did you used to be hard of yourself

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:36.860
and now you just accept it as part of the
process or how does that work?

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.750
Shawn Christensen: No, I don’t accept it.

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:43.260
I’m always hard on myself and I don’t
accept that as part of the process.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:58.900
I just…I just am sort of always rewriting
as I write and never happy with anything out

00:00:58.900 --> 00:00:59.900
of the gate.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.739
I guess what I’ll say to you is that I don’t
write very quickly in that respect.

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:12.040
I sort of inch-by-inch, word-by-word, go through
the pages and I think I’m a little bit of

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:16.270
a slower writer in that respect and other
people because if I give them a first draft

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:22.420
to somebody, the chances are it’s more like
a fifth or sixth draft, you know?

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:29.230
I’ve kind of like go the next day and rewrite
what I did the day before, then get into new

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:30.230
stuff.

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:35.479
Film Courage: Okay, so you’ll go back and
sort of revise, you won’t put in a drawer

00:01:35.479 --> 00:01:36.479
for three weeks?

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:42.640
Shawn Christensen: No, I never…If I ever
blast through pages which is rare for me.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:48.450
Like if I were to just whip out five pages,
you can be sure that the next day I will rewrite

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:52.420
those pages and then I’ll rewrite them again
the next Friday and then I’ll rewrite it.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:57.560
I’ll probably write them about eleven times
before they ever see the light of day to a

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:01.630
person reading them.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:06.050
I mean, it’s going to take seven years or
anything, but I’m always just kind of getting

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:12.410
caught up in the polishing and the making
sure of the aesthetic of the script is as

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:17.370
important as the characters and the story
and the narrative.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:26.220
You know, making sure that the pages read
like butter is very important to me and to

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:31.730
the reader, whether that be a producer or
actor or actress or financier.

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:36.730
It’s part of it and I have some reason to
go down those rabbit holes a lot.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:40.920
Film Courage: Do you think that comes from
being a musician, as well?

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.319
Shawn Christensen: Oh…I don’t know where
it comes from?

00:02:43.319 --> 00:02:47.670
I think it comes from…I don’t know?

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:54.660
I just…if a word is off or wrong or if something
doesn’t feel like, if there’s a rhythm

00:02:54.660 --> 00:03:01.480
off of the dialogue, I think this actually
more comes from improv classes.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:09.480
Because when I was in college, I really just
wanted to be an actor and when you do improv

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:16.019
and I was in a few different groups and a
few different acting coaches (one of them

00:03:16.019 --> 00:03:25.090
actually passed away a few months ago) but
when you improvise, it’s a very empowering,

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:35.310
enlightening thing and I think it’s great
if you are a writer to have that experience

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:41.430
because essentially what you can do then is
you can take two characters and really not

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:48.120
know where anything is going and let them
improvise on the page and that’s how I write

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:49.120
really.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.190
Is improvise between two characters on me.

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:56.140
Well what would I like the next person to
say, what would be nice and what’s the good

00:03:56.140 --> 00:04:01.069
rhythm and then I hate it the next day, you
know?

00:04:01.069 --> 00:04:02.859
That’s basically how it works.

